1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,866
There's a small island off the coast of Nova Scotia where people have been digging for treasure for more than two hundred years.

2
00:00:08,366 --> 00:00:10,223
Companies have gone bankrupt there.

3
00:00:10,443 --> 00:00:12,724
A future US president dug there.

4
00:00:12,944 --> 00:00:14,757
People have lost fortunes there.

5
00:00:14,977 --> 00:00:16,499
Some have lost their lives.

6
00:00:17,299 --> 00:00:22,306
The total amount of confirmed buried treasure found so far is, roughly, none.

7
00:00:22,806 --> 00:00:24,983
So why does everyone keep digging?

8
00:00:26,383 --> 00:00:29,722
Hi, I'm Archie, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

9
00:00:29,942 --> 00:00:32,004
Today, the Oak Island Money Pit.

10
00:00:32,504 --> 00:00:40,279
The longest running treasure hunt in history, and possibly the best example ever of humans refusing to give up on a hole.

11
00:00:40,779 --> 00:00:45,826
Let's separate what's documented from what's been added over two centuries of storytelling.

12
00:00:46,046 --> 00:00:50,183
Because on Oak Island, those two things are very, very far apart.

13
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:58,442
Oak Island is a small, wooded island in Mahone Bay, on the south shore of Nova Scotia in Canada.

14
00:00:58,662 --> 00:01:01,159
Today you can drive there over a causeway.

15
00:01:01,659 --> 00:01:02,933
It's privately owned.

16
00:01:03,153 --> 00:01:03,745
Pretty.

17
00:01:03,965 --> 00:01:04,580
Quiet.

18
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,966
And full of holes.

19
00:01:07,366 --> 00:01:10,024
Here's the legend, as it's usually told.

20
00:01:10,244 --> 00:01:20,737
In seventeen ninety five, a teenager on the island noticed a strange round dip in the ground, under an old oak tree with a branch that looked like it had held a pulley.

21
00:01:21,237 --> 00:01:23,778
He came back with friends and started digging.

22
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:26,794
A few feet down they hit a layer of flat stones.

23
00:01:27,014 --> 00:01:30,256
Then, every ten feet or so, a platform of logs.

24
00:01:30,756 --> 00:01:34,734
And naturally, they concluded there was pirate treasure at the bottom.

25
00:01:35,234 --> 00:01:40,432
Because when teenagers find a hole, the obvious explanation is always pirates.

26
00:01:41,832 --> 00:01:43,397
But here's the first problem.

27
00:01:43,617 --> 00:01:47,063
That story wasn't written down in seventeen ninety five.

28
00:01:47,563 --> 00:01:54,104
The earliest published accounts of the money pit appeared in the eighteen fifties, more than sixty years later.

29
00:01:54,324 --> 00:01:59,081
By then, the story had been told and retold around a lot of kitchen tables.

30
00:01:59,581 --> 00:02:09,257
So some of the most famous details, the oak tree, the pulley, the perfect platforms, may be real, may be exaggerated, or may be inventions.

31
00:02:09,477 --> 00:02:12,011
We simply don't have the original records.

32
00:02:13,411 --> 00:02:18,186
According to the nineteenth century accounts, later treasure companies dug deeper.

33
00:02:18,406 --> 00:02:19,333
Thirty feet.

34
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:20,520
Fifty feet.

35
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:21,653
Eighty feet.

36
00:02:22,153 --> 00:02:27,283
At around ninety feet, they supposedly found a stone carved with strange symbols.

37
00:02:27,503 --> 00:02:34,926
Decades later, someone claimed to translate it as, roughly, forty feet below, two million pounds are buried.

38
00:02:35,426 --> 00:02:37,164
The stone has since been lost.

39
00:02:37,384 --> 00:02:38,933
Nobody alive has seen it.

40
00:02:39,153 --> 00:02:42,576
And that translation appeared long after anyone could check.

41
00:02:43,076 --> 00:02:44,427
And then, the pit flooded.

42
00:02:44,647 --> 00:02:49,422
Seawater poured in, and the shaft filled up faster than anyone could pump it out.

43
00:02:50,822 --> 00:02:53,778
To believers, the flooding was the final proof.

44
00:02:53,998 --> 00:03:01,554
Clearly, a genius had designed flood tunnels from the beach, so that anyone who dug too deep would drown the treasure.

45
00:03:02,054 --> 00:03:14,199
Searchers have since found old wooden structures at a beach called Smith's Cove, including what some call drains, and even coconut fibre, which doesn't grow anywhere near Nova Scotia.

46
00:03:14,699 --> 00:03:18,835
Coconut fibre was often used as packing material on ships.

47
00:03:19,055 --> 00:03:20,845
So that's genuinely interesting.

48
00:03:21,065 --> 00:03:22,271
Someone brought it there.

49
00:03:22,491 --> 00:03:24,241
The question is who, and why.

50
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:29,449
Over the next century, company after company tried their luck.

51
00:03:29,669 --> 00:03:34,785
Each one dug new shafts next to the old ones, flooded those too, and went broke.

52
00:03:35,285 --> 00:03:42,933
In nineteen oh nine, one of the investors in a treasure company was a young lawyer named Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

53
00:03:43,153 --> 00:03:47,596
He kept following news from Oak Island for years, even as president.

54
00:03:48,096 --> 00:03:52,778
He went on to lead the United States through the Great Depression and a world war.

55
00:03:52,998 --> 00:03:55,199
Oak Island remained undefeated.

56
00:03:56,599 --> 00:03:57,758
It's not all funny.

57
00:03:57,978 --> 00:04:01,107
Over the years, six people have died in the search.

58
00:04:01,607 --> 00:04:10,912
The worst day came in nineteen sixty five, when four men, including a treasure hunter and his teenage son, were overcome by gas in a shaft.

59
00:04:11,412 --> 00:04:15,820
Whatever is or isn't down there, the island has taken a real toll.

60
00:04:17,220 --> 00:04:23,801
After two hundred years of digging, the original money pit can't even be located with certainty anymore.

61
00:04:24,021 --> 00:04:30,280
The area is so full of shafts, tunnels, and boreholes that it's more like Swiss cheese than an island.

62
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:35,218
The treasure hunters have basically destroyed the evidence they were looking for.

63
00:04:36,618 --> 00:04:38,528
So what's supposedly buried there?

64
00:04:38,748 --> 00:04:40,905
The list of theories is magnificent.

65
00:04:41,405 --> 00:04:43,111
Captain Kidd's pirate treasure.

66
00:04:43,331 --> 00:04:45,452
The oldest and most popular rumour.

67
00:04:45,952 --> 00:04:47,747
The treasure of the Knights Templar.

68
00:04:47,967 --> 00:04:50,100
Possibly including the Holy Grail.

69
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:54,024
The jewels of Marie Antoinette, smuggled out of France.

70
00:04:54,524 --> 00:04:59,440
And secret manuscripts proving Francis Bacon wrote the plays of Shakespeare.

71
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:05,669
At this point, the only thing nobody has suggested is a really nice parking spot.

72
00:05:07,069 --> 00:05:08,177
Now, the skeptics.

73
00:05:08,397 --> 00:05:13,511
Geologists point out that parts of Oak Island sit on limestone and gypsum.

74
00:05:14,011 --> 00:05:20,526
Those rocks dissolve in water over time, creating natural sinkholes and underground caverns.

75
00:05:20,746 --> 00:05:23,098
Caverns that connect to the sea, and flood.

76
00:05:23,598 --> 00:05:31,042
A natural sinkhole, filled with collapsed logs and debris, could look a lot like a man made shaft with platforms.

77
00:05:31,262 --> 00:05:34,286
Especially if you really, really want it to be one.

78
00:05:34,786 --> 00:05:45,769
That said, recent searches have turned up a few genuinely old objects, including coins and an old lead cross that some researchers date to before sixteen hundred.

79
00:05:45,988 --> 00:05:52,478
Whether those prove a treasure, or just centuries of people visiting an island, is still being argued.

80
00:05:53,878 --> 00:05:55,638
So here's the honest version.

81
00:05:56,138 --> 00:05:57,114
Documented.

82
00:05:57,334 --> 00:06:01,676
People have dug on Oak Island since at least the early eighteen hundreds.

83
00:06:01,896 --> 00:06:02,930
Shafts flooded.

84
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:04,484
Companies went broke.

85
00:06:04,704 --> 00:06:06,584
Franklin Roosevelt invested.

86
00:06:06,804 --> 00:06:08,245
Six people have died.

87
00:06:08,465 --> 00:06:13,776
A handful of old objects have been found, including coconut fibre and coins.

88
00:06:14,276 --> 00:06:15,006
Legend.

89
00:06:15,226 --> 00:06:25,448
The perfect platforms every ten feet, the decoded stone promising two million pounds, the booby trapped flood tunnels, and every single treasure theory.

90
00:06:25,948 --> 00:06:35,473
What probably happened is that a mysterious depression, a good story, and the human love of treasure created a loop that has lasted two centuries.

91
00:06:35,693 --> 00:06:40,031
And somewhere in there, there might still be a genuine historical puzzle.

92
00:06:40,251 --> 00:06:42,910
Just not necessarily a chest of gold.

93
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:45,362
So, be honest.

94
00:06:45,582 --> 00:06:48,160
If you owned Oak Island, would you keep digging?

95
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:49,908
Tell me in the comments.

96
00:06:50,128 --> 00:06:51,165
I'd keep digging.

97
00:06:51,385 --> 00:06:52,253
Obviously.

98
00:06:52,473 --> 00:06:53,856
I'm not made of stone.

99
00:06:54,076 --> 00:06:59,124
Unlike the missing inscribed stone, which may or may not be made of stone.

100
00:06:59,624 --> 00:07:03,768
If you like true stories that history never quite finished, subscribe.

101
00:07:03,988 --> 00:07:08,409
I'm Archie, this is The Quiet Archive, and I'll see you in the next file.
